1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,304
Saturday, two in the afternoon.

2
00:00:02,354 --> 00:00:07,104
Bekzod's hosting company takes Malika's server down for an hour of maintenance.

3
00:00:07,154 --> 00:00:10,104
In that hour, the shop sells six bouquets.

4
00:00:10,154 --> 00:00:14,712
Malika ships nothing until her server hears paid, so all six wait.

5
00:00:14,762 --> 00:00:22,896
In Lesson 29, every payment's news waited in the outbox for the relay, and in Lesson 24, Bekzod's server signed what it sent to the bank.

6
00:00:22,946 --> 00:00:28,176
This lesson delivers that news the other way, signed by the bank, to Bekzod's server.

7
00:00:28,226 --> 00:00:32,256
When the server comes back, it needs every event it missed, in order.

8
00:00:32,306 --> 00:00:37,848
It must also tell a real event from a forged one, because anyone can send a message that says paid.

9
00:00:37,898 --> 00:00:40,272
And there is a danger for the bank itself.

10
00:00:40,322 --> 00:00:47,760
Someone who signs up as a merchant could set their webhook address to a server inside the bank's own network, and make ecom call it.

11
00:00:47,810 --> 00:00:49,920
The news travels as a webhook.

12
00:00:49,970 --> 00:00:55,872
When a payment's news leaves the outbox, ecom posts it to the website's webhook address.

13
00:00:55,922 --> 00:00:59,088
That address is set by the merchant for each website.

14
00:00:59,138 --> 00:01:04,488
Every event carries its own id, the time it was sent, and a webhook signature.

15
00:01:04,538 --> 00:01:09,600
The signature is made with the same signing key as the merchant's API client.

16
00:01:09,650 --> 00:01:14,064
It follows a common open standard, so any webhook library can check it.

17
00:01:14,114 --> 00:01:20,880
Bekzod's server checks the seal, drops events that are too old, and drops any id it has seen before.

18
00:01:20,930 --> 00:01:24,288
Before connecting, ecom checks the address.

19
00:01:24,338 --> 00:01:26,424
It must be a secure address.

20
00:01:26,474 --> 00:01:28,848
It must lead only to the public internet.

21
00:01:28,898 --> 00:01:33,552
And it is checked again at the very moment of connecting, so it cannot change in between.

22
00:01:33,602 --> 00:01:35,568
Redirects are not followed.

23
00:01:35,618 --> 00:01:42,264
This guard stops server side request forgery, a trick where someone makes a server call places it should never reach.

24
00:01:42,314 --> 00:01:48,768
I would never ship webhooks without this guard, because without it, the bank's own webhooks become a way into the bank.

25
00:01:48,818 --> 00:01:53,352
A delivery succeeds only when the merchant's server answers with success.

26
00:01:53,402 --> 00:01:56,664
Anything else is tried again, waiting longer each time.

27
00:01:56,714 --> 00:02:01,224
A payment's later events wait behind an earlier one, so they stay in order.

28
00:02:01,274 --> 00:02:06,264
After the bank's chosen time, an event that never got through is marked undelivered.

29
00:02:06,314 --> 00:02:08,424
The merchant can then ask for it again.

30
00:02:08,474 --> 00:02:12,744
Payment links get no webhooks, because there is no server behind them.

31
00:02:12,794 --> 00:02:15,792
Here is that Saturday in the merchant portal.

32
00:02:15,842 --> 00:02:18,744
On a normal day, Malika opens a payment.

33
00:02:18,794 --> 00:02:22,704
Below its calls to the card scheme is a section called webhooks.

34
00:02:22,754 --> 00:02:25,320
Each event shows its type and its status.

35
00:02:25,370 --> 00:02:29,856
Here it says delivered, after one attempt, with the last answer from her server.

36
00:02:29,906 --> 00:02:32,592
Now she opens a payment from the maintenance hour.

37
00:02:32,642 --> 00:02:38,208
Its event says retrying, with the time of the next attempt and the last answer, which is no answer.

38
00:02:38,258 --> 00:02:42,096
A later event for the same payment says waiting for an earlier event.

39
00:02:42,146 --> 00:02:46,344
While an event is still retrying, there is no button to send it again.

40
00:02:46,394 --> 00:02:50,424
Suppose the maintenance runs long, past the bank's chosen time.

41
00:02:50,474 --> 00:02:52,440
The event says undelivered.

42
00:02:52,490 --> 00:02:59,232
The hint says the server never answered with success, and asks the merchant to fix it, then send the event again.

43
00:02:59,282 --> 00:03:03,168
Bekzod brings the server back, and Malika presses send again.

44
00:03:03,218 --> 00:03:06,960
A short note says it is queued and goes out within seconds.

45
00:03:07,010 --> 00:03:10,680
The status turns to delivered, and the bouquets ship.

46
00:03:10,730 --> 00:03:12,552
One last case.

47
00:03:12,602 --> 00:03:20,928
Open a payment made from a link, and the section says no webhook was sent: the website has no webhook address, or it is a payment link.

48
00:03:20,978 --> 00:03:23,784
So a forged paid never ships a bouquet.

49
00:03:23,834 --> 00:03:25,848
An outage loses no news.

50
00:03:25,898 --> 00:03:28,416
Refunded never arrives before paid.

51
00:03:28,466 --> 00:03:32,088
And the bank's own network stays out of reach of its own webhooks.

52
00:03:32,138 --> 00:03:34,512
Next: payments without a website.
